  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow clouds will likely be in place through day break across much\nof NH and portions of western Maine south of the mountains. High\npressure pressing southwestward from Canada will allow ceilings\nto lift and any lingering fog to dissipate through the morning.\nSkies will likely remain mostly cloudy through the day as low\npressure approaching from the northwest will spread additional\nclouds into the area. Highs will be in the low to mid 70s with\nsteady southeast winds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure near James Bay this evening will send a warm front\ntowards the forecast area tonight. Rain chances will increase \nwest to east overnight along with deep moisture increasing as \nPWATs climb to 1.5 inches or more. These high PWATs combined \nwith a strong southerly low level jet, some instability, and \ndeep warm clouds depths will allow for bouts of moderate rain to\nlocalized heavy rain late tonight into Thursday morning. These \nbouts of heavy rain will be scattered in coverage and short \nenough to preclude and flood concerns while the timing will \ncoincide with the Thursday morning commute that could lead to \nreduced visibility and ponding on roadways. \n\nThe low will occlude through Thursday morning as the cold front\ncatches the warm front that makes limited progress into Maine.\nThe main area of rainfall will exit through central Maine around\nmid day with CAMs suggesting portions of southern NH will get\ninto the warm sector of a secondary low forming near the triple\npoint. CAMs suggest MU CAPE will build to around 1500 J/kg \nacross southern NH early Thursday afternoon that could lead to a\nfew thunderstorms. Available forcing and dry air aloft seem to\nbe limiting coverage from latest CAMs. Will have to watch trends\nin the positioning of the warm sector as there appears to be\nenough shear for organized updrafts and potential for strong \nstorms Thursday afternoon across southern NH. Otherwise, much \nof the area will see a drying trend Thursday afternoon with \nskies trending towards mostly sunny by the evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper low over Quebec Friday will slowly cross northern Maine\nthrough Saturday. Latest model guidance is in reasonable\nagreement that Friday will be mostly dry outside of some showers\nin the mountains. Colder air aloft will move overhead Saturday\nthat will aid in destabilizing the atmosphere Saturday\nafternoon. This will allow for some instability showers to\ndevelop while PoPs generally top out around 40 percent across\nthe interior with lower chances near the coast. The upper low\nexits east Saturday night with ridging aloft building in \nSunday. This will bring mostly fair weather Sunday into Monday.",
